# .roborev.toml — TEMPLATE (controlled-build-run-kit).
#
# RoboRev's per-repo config: which agent/model reviews each commit, and the
# review guidelines it reasons against. The dial keys below port VERBATIM; the
# `review_guidelines` prose is 100% repo-specific — rewrite it for your repo.
#
# Install with `roborev init` in the target repo (it writes/uses this file and
# installs the post-commit / post-rewrite git hooks). Start the daemon with
# `roborev daemon start`.
#
# PORTING CHECKLIST:
#   1. Keep agent/review_agent = "claude-code" (RoboRev rides the Claude Code login).
#   2. Set review_model to a model your account can actually use (the reference host used
#      "claude-sonnet-4-6" — one tier below the builders, per its model dial).
#   3. review_reasoning: fast | standard | medium | thorough | maximum. "standard"
#      is a good default (a wrong finding costs a forced fix round, so don't run at minimum).
#   4. Rewrite review_guidelines for YOUR repo's principles/contracts. See

agent = "claude-code"
review_agent = "claude-code"
review_model = "claude-sonnet-4-6"
review_reasoning = "standard"
snapshot_dir = ".roborev"
review_guidelines = """
# <YOUR REPO> — review guidelines

TODO: replace this with your repo's review guidelines. Good ones:
- Name the binding docs the reviewer must read on every review (your equivalents
  of CONSTITUTION / ENGINEERING / a glossary / contracts).
- Tell it to cite a specific named principle or contract rule per finding — vague
  style commentary with no cited rule is not a finding.
- Set a severity discipline (load-bearing / minor / nitpick) and default to fewer,
  higher-quality findings.
- List what to surface (contract violations, duplication, dead code, security at
  your outside-facing seam) and what NOT to flag (your intentional patterns).
- Forbid re-litigating recorded decisions: a finding already escalated and
  recorded as declined in the plan/findings may be re-raised only by engaging
  the recorded reasoning. A checkpoint STAMP commit (record files only, diff
  only stamping the phase table plus its status records) is reviewed against
  exactly that property; any other plan/record change gets ordinary review.

"""
